The underlying root cause of the impedence mismatch here is:

a) the default 'handy is in a tray icon' behaviour. This is understandable as handy wants to get out of the way, sits in the background, and has keyboard shortcuts as its primary invocation method.
b) handy's settings is the current "main window".

I'm +1 on show tray icon option removal (at least from the Windows perspective). I read up on the commits that introduced the option, and it seemed primarily related to CLI and/or !Windows usage originally (forgive me if wrong). If thats the case, might a more refined version be to remove the option on Windows, leaving the tray option in for CLI/!Windows, if it is indeed useful there and doesnt cause UX issues like here?

FWIW, the 'standard/familiar' UX on windows is:

Main window (which handy doesnt really have at present) with taskbar icon. PS: I could imagine a cool little dockable 'main window' in Handy's future.
Option to minimize to tray. Sometimes a bonus Close to tray (either seperately or combined Minimise/Close to tray option). This allows a user to opt-in to a program living in the tray.
An open minimized option. This launches the app into the tray when (2) is enabled. This is already Handy's Launch hidden option.
Tray icon single click = do 'main' thing. Currently handy just opens context menu (not quite right, but not terrible either).
The best/most official current info for system tray behaviour on Windows lives here, and it's a good read:

https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/winenv-notification

Particular standout snippets and sections are:

Section: Is this the right user interface?
Is the icon primarily to launch a program or access its features or settings quickly? If so, use the Start menu to launch programs instead. The notification area isn't intended for quick program or command access
If Handy defaulted to taskbar only, and had an option to minimize to tray, and an option to open minimized (which it has already), thats what I'd use/set, which would match the current default behaviour, but wouldnt need the show tray icon option. This setup would also leave the option open for Handy to acquire a main window/dialog later that wasn't settings.

Edit: I just tested (for the first time), opening Handy settings from tray:

A taskbar icon is created
Minimising minimises the window, taskbar icon remains
Closing the window, closes to tray (taskbar icon disappears
My theory was both minimize and close would behave the same.

This means Handy currently has an implicit (without an option) "close to tray" i-live-in-the-background-in-the-tray default option/behaviour, again totally understandable and goes to my original impedence mismatch opening.

