I am experiencing the same issue on Windows 11: when the "Mute While Recording" option is enabled, the first one or two spoken words, for example, from a YouTube video, still end up in the transcript.

A look at the code suggests the cause lies in the sequence of operations within TranscribeAction::start (src-tauri/src/actions.rs):

Recording begins immediately (samples are captured from this point on).
Waiting for the first microphone samples (readiness).
play_feedback_sound_blocking(Start) – blocks until the signal tone finishes.
apply_mute() is called only after that.
Consequently, anything occurring between steps 1 and 4 (microphone initialization + the duration of the signal tone) is recorded without muting.

A similar gap exists when stopping: remove_mute() is called in stop() before rm.stop_recording() executes in the asynchronous task; this means audio at the end of the recording can also be captured unintentionally.

Proposed solution:

Mute immediately upon pressing the keyboard shortcut, before opening the microphone stream (the start signal tone could still be played or skipped while muted, as the microphone readiness signal is less critical when muted).
Unmute only after stop_recording() has returned the samples.
Optionally discard the first ~150 ms of samples when muted to compensate for output buffer latency. If this were implemented as an optional setting ("Instant mute via keyboard shortcut"), the problem would already be solved. I would be happy to test a build or submit a pull request (PR), if you accept them.
