Big update. Finally started debugging this with an agent using a locally compiled Handy build with extra logging. It looks like Alfred’s clipboard history is involved!

This is what the agent found from the failed paste:

Handy put the correct transcript on the clipboard and sent Cmd+V. Alfred read the new clipboard value 116 ms later. Handy treated that read as confirmation that the target app had pasted it, then restored the old clipboard after 322 ms. Alacritty hadn’t processed Cmd+V yet, so it pasted the old value instead.

macOS doesn’t tell Handy which app read the clipboard, so Alfred’s read looks the same as a read from the actual target app.

We’ve changed my local build to keep the transcript on the clipboard for 8 seconds before restoring the previous value, and I’m testing that now.

This might explain why changing the delays only helps sometimes, and why it seems to happen more under load. I guess other clipboard managers could cause the same thing too.

