There are two different failures being reported in this thread and they have the
same one-line cause: every direct-typing backend turns a character into a
keycode, and a keycode has no meaning without a layout.

Wrong characters — @konstin's Why doesn't this work? → Whz doesnät this work_.
dotool and ydotool push keycodes through uinput, and the compositor then
interprets them with whatever layout is actually loaded. The tool computed them
against a US map, the desktop reads them as QWERTZ, so y/z swap and the
punctuation lands wherever those physical keys happen to be. dotool's own man
page says as much — it "may type gobbledygook if it's simulating keycodes for a
different keyboard layout" than the environment expects.

Missing characters — Greek, Cyrillic, and accented Latin simply vanishing.
The keymap-aware backends do the opposite: they look the character up in the
keymap and find nothing, because they only search the layout group that is
active at that instant. On a us,gr setup with us active, no keycode produces
γ, so the character is dropped. enigo's libei backend makes this exact
assumption explicitly — key_to_keycode in linux/libei.rs hardcodes the
layout index to 0.

It is worth stressing that this is not about non-Latin scripts, or uppercase, or
accents as categories. It is about "not reachable from the group that is active
right now". On the same us,gr layout, € is lost exactly like γ is; flip to
the Greek group and plain a and b start disappearing instead. That is why the
German, French, Spanish, Russian and Greek reports here all behave slightly
differently while being one bug.

What actually escapes it
The only mechanism that hands the compositor a symbol instead of a keycode is
NotifyKeyboardKeysym on the xdg-desktop-portal RemoteDesktop interface, which
is what #689 builds on. It is the right direction, but on current desktops it
does not get you all the way:

KWin ≥ 6.6 synthesizes a scratch keymap for symbols outside the active
group, so it works — except in Chromium and Electron applications. KWin sends
the key on evdev keycode 247, Chromium translates evdev codes to DomCode
through a fixed table that has no row for 247, and
WaylandKeyboard::DispatchKey returns early on DomCode::NONE. The keystroke
disappears with no error anywhere. So Greek types fine into Konsole and system
dialogs and not at all into Chrome, VS Code or Slack. That is a one-constant
fix and I have it in review:
https://invent.kde.org/plasma/kwin/-/merge_requests/9755
mutter drops out-of-group keysyms outright.
pick_keycode_for_keyval_in_current_group_in_impl scans only
XKB_STATE_LAYOUT_EFFECTIVE and logs No keycode found for keyval X in current group; the injection is queued to another thread, so the D-Bus reply is sent
before the lookup and the caller is told it succeeded. Open upstream since 2021
and 2022 as mutter#1802
and mutter#2111, where I
have added the current analysis and a reproducer. There is no client-side way
around it either: mutter does not implement zwp_virtual_keyboard_manager_v1,
which is the protocol wtype needs — that is [BUG] wtype failures seem silently ignored #522 here.
wlroots compositors (sway, Hyprland) have no RemoteDesktop portal at all,
but they are the one family that does implement
zwp_virtual_keyboard_manager_v1, which is why wtype works there and nowhere
else. Exactly the inverse of KDE and GNOME. (That one is from the protocol
support rather than my own testing — I only have KDE hardware here.)
There is one piece of good news for GNOME, though not yet in a release.
https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/merge_requests/4996 adds libei's text
capability to mutter, which has two halves: a keysym event, which routes into
the broken lookup above, and a utf8 event, which takes a completely different
path — CLUTTER_IM_COMMIT, i.e. the input-method route, with no keycode
resolution anywhere. That half can carry arbitrary text. It is worth watching,
because it would give GNOME a real answer rather than a workaround, and Handy is
already on libei through enigo. (Its keysym half inheriting the bug is something
I have flagged on the MR.)

The practical answer today
@cjpais is right that Direct is not great, though I would put it as a limit on
what the application can promise rather than on the approach. Direct typing is
the nicer behaviour when the compositor cooperates — it leaves the clipboard
alone and does not care what the target application binds paste to — and on KDE
it does cooperate, or will once the MR above lands. What Handy cannot do is make
that true on mutter or on wlroots, so it cannot be the path everything falls back
to.

Clipboard plus a paste chord is the only path that is independent of both the
layout and the compositor — X11 included. It is not free of caveats: the chord
still has to match what the target application uses, so terminals need
Ctrl+Shift+V rather than Ctrl+V, which is presumably why the setting exists.
But it fails visibly and in a way the user can fix, rather than dropping
characters silently.

One detail matters for it: the chord has to be sent as evdev keycodes
(KEY_LEFTCTRL 29, KEY_V 47), not as keysyms. Physical keycodes exist in every
layout; a keysym for v does not, once the active group is Greek or Cyrillic. I
measured what each compositor then does to a keysym Ctrl+v, and both break it
silently, in different shapes:

KWin routes the v through the scratch keymap, and the keymap swap
force-releases the modifier that is being held — so the chord degrades into
typing a bare v into the document.
mutter drops the v outright (No keycode found for keyval 76 in current group) and nothing at all reaches the client. Modifier handling itself is
clean — Control_L exists in every group, so it presses and releases
correctly — the chord simply has no second half.
The same run also confirms the symmetry mentioned above: with the Greek group
active, a plain b sent as a keysym is dropped by mutter exactly like γ is
with the Latin group active.

That also means the Ctrl+Shift+U Unicode fallback in #689 will not rescue the
cases it is meant to: it is an IBus/GTK convention, so it does nothing in Qt
applications, most terminals, or Chromium and Electron apps — the same set that
loses the keysym path today. Replacing it with a clipboard fallback for any
character the active layout cannot produce would close this issue for everyone
rather than for KDE users, and it needs no new platform support.

I am on a us,gr KDE Plasma 6.7.4 setup and happy to test patches, or to run
the same measurements on someone else's layout if that helps narrow a specific
report here.


