I retested this on current main (37a26fd, version 0.9.5) on Windows 11 26200 and measured what the OS actually does to us. The freeze is gone — I could not reproduce a hang in any of the three paths below. But the underlying cause is still there and now produces two different, quieter failures, so I think this issue needs re-describing rather than closing.

What the OS does
I wrote a small probe against handy-keys 0.3.3 (the version this repo pins) that puts three streams on one timeline: what the LL hook delivers, what GetAsyncKeyState reports, and which window is foreground. Same key pair as the report, Ctrl+Space, unelevated process, elevated Notepad as the target.

Control — normal window focused, keys physically held:

71346  BEAT  fg=Notepad.exe elevated=no  ctrl=true
72353  BEAT  fg=Notepad.exe elevated=no  ctrl=true
       ...continuous HOOK key=Space down=true auto-repeat
Case — elevated window focused, same keys physically held, 7 seconds:

98494  BEAT  fg=Notepad.exe elevated=yes ctrl=false
99504  BEAT  fg=Notepad.exe elevated=yes ctrl=false
       ...zero HOOK events
The part I did not expect: GetAsyncKeyState goes blind too, not just the hook. ctrl is read identically in both runs and reports false while the key is physically down. So while an elevated window has focus, an unelevated process has no way to observe keyboard state — neither the hook nor a physical-state poll.

That matters for the repair primitive already in handy-keys: reconcile_modifiers() is built on GetAsyncKeyState, so it cannot reconcile anything during elevated focus. Its existing trigger (WM_POWERBROADCAST / WM_WTSSESSION_CHANGE) never fires here either.

One thing is not blind: GetForegroundWindow keeps working, and OpenProcessToken failing on the foreground process is itself a reliable signal for "input from here will not reach me".

What that produces today
1. Push-to-talk release is never delivered, so the recording is unbounded. Press over a normal window, switch to the elevated window while holding, release. Nothing happens. The release only lands when focus leaves the elevated window:

11:43:28 handy-keys event: binding=transcribe, hotkey=ctrl+space, state=Pressed
11:43:29 Recording started for binding transcribe
         ... focus moved to elevated Notepad, key physically released, no event
11:43:48 handy-keys event: binding=transcribe, hotkey=ctrl+space, state=Released
11:43:48 Recording stopped and samples retrieved, sample count: 237120
20 seconds between the press and the release finally landing, with the key physically let go somewhere in the middle — the recording ran for all of it, and would have kept going indefinitely had focus stayed on the elevated window. This is @giuliofoletto's "switching focus while recording" case: it no longer freezes, it strands.

2. The transcript is delivered to the wrong window. It pastes wherever focus landed when the release finally arrived, not where the user was speaking.

3. Starting over an elevated window does nothing at all, silently. No handy-keys event is logged, no overlay, no feedback. The RELEASE_GRACE/DEBOUNCE handling in TranscriptionCoordinator never sees an event to work with.

The original report's log (two ~100ms recordings, then a hang) does not match any of this, which I think is simply because the coordinator, reliable-paste and secure_input work all landed after December.

Where I think the fix goes, and the question
Since the foreground window is observable, the state can be bounded: while a push-to-talk recording is active and focus moves to a window whose input we cannot receive, the release is guaranteed never to arrive, so continuing is guaranteed wrong.

What should happen at that moment is a behaviour call I would rather not make unilaterally:

Stop and transcribe, treating the focus change as the release — keeps the user's speech, but the paste target is then the elevated window, where it will fail by design.
Cancel and surface why — no wrong-window paste and no stranded recording, at the cost of discarding what was said.
There is also the separate, smaller question of whether case 3 deserves any feedback at all, or whether silence is acceptable there.

Happy to send a PR once you say which of those you want. Worth noting the detection may belong in handy-keys rather than here, since that is where the input observability is known — I can go either way.

Tested on Windows 11 Home 26200.9168, handy-keys 0.3.3, keyboard_implementation: HandyKeys, push_to_talk: true. I have not reproduced on any other Windows build, and the original reporter's freeze may well still exist on older ones.

AI disclosure: I used Claude Code for this investigation — it wrote the probe, read the logs with me, and drafted this comment. The measurements and the reproduction are from my machine.


