I can confirm Giulio Foletto's observations.
In my opinion, the fact that Handy is freezing when the overlay is enabled over an elevated app can be considered a bug.

However, it seems normal that an app not elevated can't interfere with an admin window. For example, as a user of Ditto (a clipboard manager), when you try to paste from Ditto into an elevated window, Ditto will prompt to run an elevated task, launching its own binary with elevated privileges, which then allows pasting into the elevated window. They call this "UAC helper."
You can observe a similar behavior with an AutoHotkey script.
Any workaround to bypass this behavior would create a potential security breach.

So, the best solution would probably be to fix the freezing bug first.
Then, for the scenario where "Handy (not elevated)" interacts with an "elevated Notepad" window, Handy could display a message (e.g., "I’m not going to make it" 😄) when an elevated target window is detected.

Edit:
After further consideration, here’s an ideal approach for handling an elevated window, assuming we don’t want to cancel the current transcription:

Detect if the target window is elevated.
The overlay alerts the user:
"⚠ Handy cannot type in an elevated UAC window directly."
At the end of the transcription, the overlay displays a button to copy the content to the clipboard, with the message:
"⚠ Handy cannot type in an elevated UAC window directly. Press this button to copy the current content to the clipboard, then manually paste it into your elevated window."
The user can then manually press Ctrl + V to paste the content.
It might not be the easiest to implement 😅, but this approach will guide the user through the process step by step.

